# Evidence-command allowlist (ADR-009). One bare command name per line.
#
# Evidence commands re-execute later inside VERIFIER sessions (recheck),
# so everything here must be read-only: it will run with whatever the
# repository can read, in a session that did not write it. The screen
# also refuses output redirection (except to /dev/null or an fd dup
# like 2>&1), command substitution ('$()', backticks), subshells, and
# path-form programs ('./x', '/usr/bin/x') regardless of this list.
# The screen is quote-aware: a '|' inside a quoted regex is an argument.
# Pipelines of allowlisted commands pass; input redirection ('<') is
# read-only and passes.
#
# Since ADR-041 an evidence command NEVER reaches /bin/sh: the screen's
# parse is what executes, as argv arrays with shell=False, and the runner
# does the pipes, '&&'/'||'/';', '>/dev/null', '2>&1' and '<FILE' itself.
# Constructs with no argv equivalent are refused rather than
# approximated: '$VAR' and '~' expansion, '<>', '&' backgrounding, and a
# glob in program position. A quoted metacharacter stays a literal, and
# an unmatched pattern passes through as one, exactly as the shell did.
#
# Test runners (pytest, npm test, cargo test, make) are deliberately NOT
# shipped here: a test runner executes repository code, which is exactly
# the arbitrary-execution channel being screened. Add yours below as a
# conscious, committed policy decision if you accept that trade.
#
# git is deliberately NOT shipped and MUST NOT be added for evidence use
# (ADR-021, H4). Its exec/write surface is unbounded by a flag blocklist:
# `git filter-branch --tree-filter <cmd>` is arbitrary code execution,
# and archive -o / bundle / config --file / worktree / format-patch -o /
# checkout-index / bisect run / submodule foreach / `-c <k>=!cmd` aliases
# are further exec-or-write channels. PROGRAM_ARG_DENY blunts a handful of
# top-level git flags but CANNOT make git safe -- do not rely on it. find
# is shipped and its known write/exec flags (-exec/-execdir/-ok/-okdir/
# -delete/-fprint*/-fls) are refused by that same table. sort is shipped
# but its table entry is NOT complete: the 2026-08-01 audit (ADR-040)
# confirmed `sort --out=FILE` and `--com=PROG` -- getopt accepts any
# unambiguous long-option abbreviation, while the table lists one
# spelling. Treat the table as a blunting layer, never a boundary, and
# prefer minimal allowlists.
#
# rg / file / date were REMOVED from this default on 2026-08-01 (ADR-040)
# after a per-program audit of every entry: `rg --pre PROG` and
# `--hostname-bin PROG` execute arbitrary programs, `file -C -m PATH`
# writes a compiled magic file to an arbitrary path, and `date` sets the
# system clock from a flag (GNU -s/--set) or a bare POSITIONAL (BSD).
# All three are now in DOCTOR_GREY_ZONE, so `truth doctor` warns any
# consumer whose own allowlist still carries them. Re-add only as a
# conscious, committed decision.
grep
ls
cat
head
tail
wc
find
sort
uniq
cut
tr
diff
comm
echo
printf
test
[
basename
dirname
realpath
stat
sha256sum
shasum
md5sum
jq
# sed and awk are NOT shipped: `sed -i` and awk output redirection write
# files without a '>' the screen could see. Add them only if your
# conventions forbid those forms.
